{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/kodd/Nutri","sessionId":"d0a4426d-7a3e-4469-b77d-2093bf639a97","version":"2.1.31","gitBranch":"HEAD","agentId":"a886047","slug":"purring-mixing-harp","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are helping design a universal health tracking system. The core model is:\n\n**Item** (what you track):\n- name, type (input|output|context), value_type (number|boolean|scale|text|select), unit, options\n\n**Entry** (logged value):\n- item_id, value, timestamp, notes\n\nThe correlation engine finds patterns between inputs/contexts → outputs.\n\nYOUR TASK: Generate 15-20 detailed user scenarios for people with SPECIFIC HEALTH CONDITIONS. For each scenario:\n\n1. **User profile** (condition, goals)\n2. **Items they'd track** (list 5-10 items with full properties: name, type, value_type, unit)\n3. **Sample entries** (a week of realistic data)\n4. **Correlations the engine might discover**\n5. **Edge cases or challenges** this user might encounter with the model\n\nCover diverse conditions:\n- Digestive issues (IBS, SIBO, food sensitivities)\n- Autoimmune (Hashimoto's, RA, lupus)\n- Metabolic (diabetes, insulin resistance)\n- Mental health (anxiety, depression, ADHD)\n- Chronic fatigue / fibromyalgia\n- Migraines\n- Skin conditions (eczema, acne)\n- Hormonal issues (PCOS, thyroid, menopause)\n- Sleep disorders\n- Chronic pain\n\nBe extremely detailed and realistic. Think about what these users ACTUALLY deal with day to day."},"uuid":"2ed543ca-02a7-4386-811f-7707d311c793","timestamp":"2026-02-04T03:54:24.526Z"}
{"parentUuid":"2ed543ca-02a7-4386-811f-7707d311c793","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/kodd/Nutri","sessionId":"d0a4426d-7a3e-4469-b77d-2093bf639a97","version":"2.1.31","gitBranch":"HEAD","agentId":"a886047","slug":"purring-mixing-harp","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_018nSergj6kgsuTxw7Pishqg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Universal Health Tracking System: Detailed User Scenarios\n\n## Scenario 1: IBS with FODMAP Sensitivity\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Sarah, 34, Software Engineer  \n**Condition:** IBS-D (diarrhea-predominant) with suspected FODMAP sensitivity  \n**Goals:** Identify trigger foods, reduce flare-ups from 4x/week to 1x/week, regain confidence eating out\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Garlic consumed | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Onion consumed | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Lactose consumed | input | select | - | none, trace, moderate, high |\n| Fiber intake | input | number | grams | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Sleep quality | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Bloating severity | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Bowel movements | output | number | count | - |\n| Stool consistency | output | select | - | Bristol 1-7 |\n| Abdominal pain | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Urgency episodes | output | number | count | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Garlic: true, Onion: false, Lactose: none, Fiber: 18g, Stress: 4, Sleep: 7\n- Bloating: 3, Bowel movements: 2, Stool: Bristol 5, Pain: 2, Urgency: 0\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Garlic: true, Onion: true, Lactose: trace, Fiber: 24g, Stress: 6, Sleep: 5\n- Bloating: 7, Bowel movements: 4, Stool: Bristol 6, Pain: 5, Urgency: 2\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Garlic: false, Onion: false, Lactose: none, Fiber: 15g, Stress: 7, Sleep: 4\n- Bloating: 6, Bowel movements: 3, Stool: Bristol 5, Pain: 4, Urgency: 1\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Garlic: false, Onion: false, Lactose: moderate, Fiber: 20g, Stress: 3, Sleep: 8\n- Bloating: 5, Bowel movements: 3, Stool: Bristol 5, Pain: 3, Urgency: 1\n\n**Friday:**\n- Garlic: false, Onion: false, Lactose: none, Fiber: 16g, Stress: 2, Sleep: 8\n- Bloating: 2, Bowel movements: 2, Stool: Bristol 4, Pain: 1, Urgency: 0\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Garlic: true, Onion: true, Lactose: high, Fiber: 28g, Stress: 4, Sleep: 7\n- Bloating: 9, Bowel movements: 5, Stool: Bristol 6, Pain: 7, Urgency: 3\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Garlic: false, Onion: false, Lactose: none, Fiber: 14g, Stress: 2, Sleep: 9\n- Bloating: 4, Bowel movements: 2, Stool: Bristol 4, Pain: 2, Urgency: 0\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Onion + Garlic combination** → 85% correlation with bloating >6 (24-48hr delayed effect)\n2. **Stress >5 + Poor sleep (<6)** → 70% correlation with next-day symptoms regardless of diet\n3. **Fiber >22g** → 60% correlation with increased urgency (but only when combined with FODMAP triggers)\n4. **Lactose \"high\"** → 78% correlation with Bristol 6-7 within 4 hours\n5. **2+ days low-FODMAP + good sleep** → 90% correlation with symptom-free days\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Delayed reactions:** Symptoms from Tuesday's garlic may not appear until Wednesday afternoon. The model needs to look at 24-72 hour windows, not just same-day correlations.\n\n2. **Stacking effects:** Garlic alone might be tolerable, but garlic + onion + stress creates a compounding effect. Simple pairwise correlations miss this.\n\n3. **Threshold effects:** 15g of fiber is fine, 22g is borderline, 28g causes issues. Linear models won't capture the cliff edge.\n\n4. **Ingredient uncertainty:** Sarah eats restaurant food on Saturday but can't confirm if there was hidden garlic in the sauce. Need a \"suspected\" or \"uncertain\" option.\n\n5. **Period cycle interaction:** Her symptoms are worse during luteal phase. Without cycle tracking integration, patterns appear random 1 week per month.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 2: Hashimoto's Thyroiditis\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Michael, 42, High School Teacher  \n**Condition:** Hashimoto's thyroiditis, on levothyroxine 88mcg  \n**Goals:** Optimize medication timing, identify inflammation triggers, stabilize energy levels\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Levothyroxine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Time before eating after meds | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Coffee timing post-meds | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Gluten consumed | input | select | - | none, trace, moderate, high |\n| Selenium supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Iron-rich meal timing | input | select | - | none, with-meds, 2hr-after, 4hr-after |\n| Morning body temp | context | number | °F | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Energy level | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Brain fog | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Joint stiffness | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Cold sensitivity | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Hair shedding | output | select | - | normal, slightly-increased, significant |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 60, Coffee: 65, Gluten: none, Selenium: true, Iron timing: 4hr-after\n- Temp: 97.8, Sleep: 7.5\n- Energy: 7, Brain fog: 2, Joints: 2, Cold: false, Hair: normal\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 30, Coffee: 35, Gluten: trace, Selenium: true, Iron timing: 2hr-after\n- Temp: 97.6, Sleep: 6\n- Energy: 5, Brain fog: 4, Joints: 3, Cold: true, Hair: normal\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 60, Coffee: 120, Gluten: moderate, Selenium: false, Iron timing: 4hr-after\n- Temp: 97.4, Sleep: 7\n- Energy: 4, Brain fog: 5, Joints: 5, Cold: true, Hair: slightly-increased\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 45, Coffee: 50, Gluten: high, Selenium: true, Iron timing: with-meds\n- Temp: 97.2, Sleep: 8\n- Energy: 3, Brain fog: 7, Joints: 6, Cold: true, Hair: slightly-increased\n\n**Friday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 60, Coffee: 90, Gluten: none, Selenium: true, Iron timing: 4hr-after\n- Temp: 97.5, Sleep: 7\n- Energy: 5, Brain fog: 4, Joints: 4, Cold: true, Hair: slightly-increased\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 75, Coffee: 180, Gluten: none, Selenium: true, Iron timing: none\n- Temp: 97.7, Sleep: 9\n- Energy: 7, Brain fog: 2, Joints: 2, Cold: false, Hair: normal\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Levo: true, Wait before food: 90, Coffee: 180, Gluten: none, Selenium: true, Iron timing: 4hr-after\n- Temp: 97.9, Sleep: 8.5\n- Energy: 8, Brain fog: 1, Joints: 1, Cold: false, Hair: normal\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Coffee <60min after meds** → 65% reduction in next-day energy (interferes with absorption)\n2. **Gluten moderate/high** → 72% correlation with brain fog >4 and joint stiffness >4 within 48-72 hours\n3. **Iron with-meds or 2hr-after** → 55% correlation with lower energy (blocks absorption)\n4. **Wait before food <45min** → 60% correlation with afternoon energy crash\n5. **3+ consecutive gluten-free days** → 80% correlation with energy >6, brain fog <3\n6. **Body temp <97.4** → 85% correlation with cold sensitivity and energy <5 (indicates under-medication or absorption issues)\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Long feedback loops:** Thyroid medication changes take 4-6 weeks to stabilize. The correlation engine needs to handle very long lag times for medication adjustments.\n\n2. **Seasonal variation:** Michael's symptoms are worse in winter. The model might incorrectly attribute this to diet changes when it's actually reduced sunlight and vitamin D.\n\n3. **Lab result integration:** Without TSH/T4/antibody levels, the model can't distinguish between \"inflammation flare\" and \"needs dose adjustment.\" Consider adding periodic lab entries.\n\n4. **Medication brand switches:** His pharmacy switched to generic one month. Symptoms changed but he didn't think to track \"brand.\" Need medication brand as an item.\n\n5. **Cross-reactivity confusion:** Gluten and dairy often correlate because people eat them together (pizza, sandwiches). Model might identify wrong trigger.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 3: Type 2 Diabetes with Insulin Resistance\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Patricia, 56, Retired Accountant  \n**Condition:** Type 2 diabetes (diagnosed 3 years), on metformin 1000mg 2x daily, A1C currently 7.2%  \n**Goals:** Reduce A1C to <6.5%, minimize glucose spikes, understand individual food responses\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Carbohydrate intake | input | number | grams | - |\n| Protein intake | input | number | grams | - |\n| Fat intake | input | number | grams | - |\n| Meal composition order | input | select | - | veggies-first, protein-first, carbs-first, mixed |\n| Walking after meal | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Metformin taken | input | select | - | with-breakfast, with-dinner, both, missed-one, missed-both |\n| Fasting glucose | output | number | mg/dL | - |\n| Post-meal glucose (2hr) | output | number | mg/dL | - |\n| Time in range (CGM) | output | number | percent | - |\n| Neuropathy tingling | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Menstrual cycle day | context | number | day | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Carbs: 120g, Protein: 75g, Fat: 60g, Order: mixed, Walk: 0, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 6, Stress: 5, Cycle: n/a (post-menopausal)\n- Fasting: 142, Post-meal avg: 185, Time in range: 62%, Tingling: 3\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Carbs: 85g, Protein: 90g, Fat: 70g, Order: veggies-first, Walk: 15, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 7, Stress: 4\n- Fasting: 128, Post-meal avg: 158, Time in range: 74%, Tingling: 2\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Carbs: 95g, Protein: 85g, Fat: 65g, Order: protein-first, Walk: 20, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 7.5, Stress: 3\n- Fasting: 118, Post-meal avg: 145, Time in range: 81%, Tingling: 1\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Carbs: 150g, Protein: 60g, Fat: 55g, Order: carbs-first, Walk: 0, Metformin: missed-one\n- Sleep: 5, Stress: 7\n- Fasting: 156, Post-meal avg: 210, Time in range: 48%, Tingling: 5\n\n**Friday:**\n- Carbs: 75g, Protein: 100g, Fat: 80g, Order: veggies-first, Walk: 25, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 8, Stress: 3\n- Fasting: 115, Post-meal avg: 138, Time in range: 85%, Tingling: 1\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Carbs: 130g, Protein: 70g, Fat: 60g, Order: mixed, Walk: 10, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 7, Stress: 4\n- Fasting: 135, Post-meal avg: 172, Time in range: 68%, Tingling: 3\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Carbs: 80g, Protein: 95g, Fat: 75g, Order: protein-first, Walk: 30, Metformin: both\n- Sleep: 8.5, Stress: 2\n- Fasting: 108, Post-meal avg: 132, Time in range: 88%, Tingling: 0\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Veggies-first or protein-first meal order** → 28% lower post-meal glucose spike on average\n2. **Walking >15min after meal** → 35% reduction in 2-hour post-meal glucose\n3. **Carbs >100g + no post-meal walk** → 90% chance of post-meal >180\n4. **Sleep <6 hours** → next-day fasting glucose elevated by average 15 mg/dL\n5. **Stress >6** → 22% higher glucose readings regardless of diet\n6. **Missed metformin dose** → 40% reduction in time-in-range for 24-48 hours\n7. **Consecutive days of <85g carbs + walking** → 15% improvement in weekly fasting average\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Individual food responses:** Patricia spikes 60 points from white rice but only 25 from sourdough bread at same carb count. The model needs food-specific tracking, not just macros.\n\n2. **Dawn phenomenon:** Her fasting glucose is often higher than bedtime glucose due to liver glucose dump overnight. This pattern isn't caused by any input she controls.\n\n3. **CGM calibration issues:** Some days her CGM reads 20 points higher than fingerstick. Need a way to mark \"sensor day 1\" or \"sensor issues\" as context.\n\n4. **Medication timing precision:** Taking metformin \"with dinner\" at 6pm vs 9pm has different effects. Need timestamp, not just boolean.\n\n5. **Cumulative effects:** One high-carb day is recoverable. Three in a row creates a multi-day elevated baseline. The model needs to detect streaks.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 4: Generalized Anxiety Disorder\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** James, 28, Graphic Designer  \n**Condition:** Generalized Anxiety Disorder, currently on sertraline 50mg  \n**Goals:** Identify anxiety triggers, optimize medication timing, reduce panic attacks from 2x/week to <1x/month\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Caffeine intake | input | number | mg | - |\n| Caffeine cutoff time | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Sertraline taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Alcohol units | input | number | units | - |\n| Exercise duration | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Exercise type | input | select | - | cardio, strength, yoga, walk, none |\n| Screen time before bed | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Morning anxiety | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Afternoon anxiety | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Evening anxiety | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Panic attack | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Heart palpitations | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Racing thoughts | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Sleep quality | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Work deadline | context | boolean | - | - |\n| Social event | context | boolean | - | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Caffeine: 200mg, Cutoff: 14, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 0, Exercise: 30 (cardio), Screen: 45\n- Sleep: 7, Deadline: true, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 6, Afternoon: 7, Evening: 5, Panic: false, Palpitations: 4, Racing thoughts: 6\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Caffeine: 300mg, Cutoff: 16, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 2, Exercise: 0 (none), Screen: 90\n- Sleep: 5, Deadline: true, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 7, Afternoon: 8, Evening: 7, Panic: true, Palpitations: 7, Racing thoughts: 8\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Caffeine: 100mg, Cutoff: 12, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 0, Exercise: 45 (yoga), Screen: 30\n- Sleep: 6, Deadline: true, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 5, Afternoon: 5, Evening: 4, Panic: false, Palpitations: 3, Racing thoughts: 4\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Caffeine: 150mg, Cutoff: 14, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 0, Exercise: 20 (walk), Screen: 60\n- Sleep: 7.5, Deadline: false, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 4, Afternoon: 4, Evening: 3, Panic: false, Palpitations: 2, Racing thoughts: 3\n\n**Friday:**\n- Caffeine: 250mg, Cutoff: 15, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 4, Exercise: 0, Screen: 120\n- Sleep: 6, Deadline: false, Social: true\n- Morning anxiety: 5, Afternoon: 6, Evening: 8, Panic: false, Palpitations: 5, Racing thoughts: 7\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Caffeine: 100mg, Cutoff: 11, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 3, Exercise: 60 (cardio), Screen: 40\n- Sleep: 5 (hangover), Deadline: false, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 7, Afternoon: 5, Evening: 4, Panic: false, Palpitations: 4, Racing thoughts: 5\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Caffeine: 50mg, Cutoff: 10, Sertraline: true, Alcohol: 0, Exercise: 30 (yoga), Screen: 20\n- Sleep: 8.5, Deadline: false, Social: false\n- Morning anxiety: 2, Afternoon: 2, Evening: 2, Panic: false, Palpitations: 1, Racing thoughts: 2\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Caffeine >200mg + cutoff after 14:00** → 75% correlation with evening anxiety >5 and poor sleep\n2. **Alcohol >2 units** → 80% correlation with next-day anxiety increase of +2-3 points (\"hangxiety\")\n3. **Yoga** → 60% reduction in same-day anxiety compared to rest days\n4. **Screen time >60min before bed** → 65% correlation with morning anxiety >5\n5. **No exercise + work deadline** → 85% correlation with panic attack within 48 hours\n6. **Sleep <6 hours** → 70% correlation with palpitations >4 next day\n7. **3+ consecutive low-anxiety days** → 90% reduction in panic attack risk\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Anticipatory anxiety:** James feels anxious on Sunday about Monday's deadline, but there's no \"anticipated deadline\" item. Need forward-looking context items.\n\n2. **Social anxiety specifics:** \"Social event\" is too broad. A party with strangers vs dinner with close friends have opposite effects. Need granularity.\n\n3. **Medication adjustment periods:** He increased sertraline from 25mg to 50mg two weeks ago. Initial weeks showed increased anxiety (normal adjustment). Model might incorrectly correlate higher dose with worse outcomes.\n\n4. **Panic attack unpredictability:** Some panic attacks have no identifiable trigger. The model shouldn't force false patterns.\n\n5. **Subjective scale drift:** His \"5\" anxiety today might be different from his \"5\" three months ago as he calibrates differently over time.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 5: Chronic Migraine\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Elena, 38, Marketing Manager  \n**Condition:** Chronic migraine (15+ headache days/month), with aura, on topiramate 100mg daily  \n**Goals:** Reduce migraine frequency to <8 days/month, identify prodrome signs, optimize acute medication timing\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Water intake | input | number | oz | - |\n| Skipped meals | input | number | count | - |\n| Tyramine foods | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Alcohol type | input | select | - | none, red-wine, white-wine, beer, spirits |\n| Topiramate taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Sumatriptan used | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Neck tension | context | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Barometric pressure change | context | select | - | stable, rising, falling, storm-front |\n| Menstrual cycle day | context | number | day | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Screen time | context | number | hours | - |\n| Headache intensity | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Aura present | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Nausea | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Light sensitivity | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Migraine duration | output | number | hours | - |\n| Functional ability | output | select | - | normal, reduced, bed-bound |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Water: 64oz, Skipped meals: 0, Tyramine: false, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: false\n- Neck tension: 3, Pressure: stable, Cycle day: 8, Sleep: 7, Screen: 8\n- Headache: 2, Aura: false, Nausea: 0, Light sens: 1, Duration: 0, Function: normal\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Water: 48oz, Skipped meals: 1, Tyramine: true, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: false\n- Neck tension: 5, Pressure: falling, Cycle day: 9, Sleep: 6, Screen: 10\n- Headache: 4, Aura: false, Nausea: 2, Light sens: 3, Duration: 4, Function: reduced\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Water: 32oz, Skipped meals: 1, Tyramine: false, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: true\n- Neck tension: 7, Pressure: storm-front, Cycle day: 10, Sleep: 5, Screen: 6\n- Headache: 8, Aura: true, Nausea: 7, Light sens: 9, Duration: 12, Function: bed-bound\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Water: 72oz, Skipped meals: 0, Tyramine: false, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: false\n- Neck tension: 4, Pressure: rising, Cycle day: 11, Sleep: 9, Screen: 4\n- Headache: 3, Aura: false, Nausea: 1, Light sens: 2, Duration: 0, Function: normal\n\n**Friday:**\n- Water: 56oz, Skipped meals: 0, Tyramine: false, Alcohol: red-wine, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: false\n- Neck tension: 4, Pressure: stable, Cycle day: 12, Sleep: 7, Screen: 7\n- Headache: 3, Aura: false, Nausea: 1, Light sens: 2, Duration: 0, Function: normal\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Water: 40oz, Skipped meals: 0, Tyramine: true, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: true\n- Neck tension: 6, Pressure: stable, Cycle day: 13, Sleep: 8, Screen: 5\n- Headache: 7, Aura: true, Nausea: 6, Light sens: 8, Duration: 8, Function: bed-bound\n\n**Sunday (Cycle day 26 - different week for menstrual migraine):**\n- Water: 64oz, Skipped meals: 0, Tyramine: false, Alcohol: none, Topiramate: true, Sumatriptan: true\n- Neck tension: 5, Pressure: stable, Cycle day: 26, Sleep: 7, Screen: 4\n- Headache: 8, Aura: false, Nausea: 5, Light sens: 7, Duration: 14, Function: bed-bound\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Cycle days 25-28 and 1-2** → 85% correlation with migraine (menstrual migraine pattern)\n2. **Neck tension >5** → 70% probability of migraine within 24 hours (prodrome indicator)\n3. **Skipped meals + low water (<48oz)** → 65% correlation with migraine within 12 hours\n4. **Barometric pressure \"falling\" or \"storm-front\"** → 55% increased migraine risk\n5. **Red wine** → 72% correlation with next-day migraine (but white wine shows no correlation)\n6. **Tyramine + any other trigger** → synergistic effect, 80% migraine probability\n7. **Sleep deviation >2 hours from baseline** → 45% increased risk (both over and under-sleeping)\n8. **Screen time >9 hours** → 40% correlation with tension headache escalating to migraine\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Prodrome vs trigger confusion:** Neck tension might be a prodrome (migraine already starting) rather than a trigger. The model needs to distinguish warning signs from causes.\n\n2. **Medication overuse headache:** If Elena uses sumatriptan >10 days/month, she risks rebound headaches. The model should flag this pattern.\n\n3. **Multi-trigger stacking:** One trigger rarely causes a migraine alone. It's the combination of falling pressure + poor sleep + skipped meal that does it. Need combinatorial analysis.\n\n4. **Hormonal contraceptive interaction:** If she starts/stops birth control, her menstrual migraine pattern will shift. Need to track medication changes.\n\n5. **Weather data accuracy:** She can't control barometric pressure tracking manually. Need integration with weather API or allow \"I think a storm is coming\" subjective entry.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 6: ADHD (Adult, Combined Type)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Marcus, 32, Freelance Writer  \n**Condition:** ADHD combined type, on Vyvanse 40mg  \n**Goals:** Optimize medication timing, understand focus patterns, reduce hyperfocus crashes\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Vyvanse taken time | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Vyvanse with protein | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Coffee cups | input | number | count | - |\n| Vitamin C timing | input | select | - | morning, afternoon, evening, none |\n| Exercise before work | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Deadline pressure | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Task novelty | context | select | - | new-exciting, routine, tedious |\n| Morning focus | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Afternoon focus | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Evening focus | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Hyperfocus episode | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Task switching ease | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Emotional regulation | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Words written | output | number | count | - |\n| Medication crash | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Vyvanse: 7:00, Protein: true, Coffee: 2, Vitamin C: evening, Exercise: true\n- Sleep: 7.5, Deadline: 3, Novelty: new-exciting\n- Morning focus: 9, Afternoon: 7, Evening: 4, Hyperfocus: true, Task switch: 6, Emotional: 7, Words: 3200, Crash: 3\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Vyvanse: 9:00, Protein: false, Coffee: 3, Vitamin C: morning, Exercise: false\n- Sleep: 6, Deadline: 5, Novelty: routine\n- Morning focus: 5, Afternoon: 6, Evening: 3, Hyperfocus: false, Task switch: 4, Emotional: 5, Words: 1100, Crash: 6\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Vyvanse: 7:30, Protein: true, Coffee: 1, Vitamin C: evening, Exercise: true\n- Sleep: 8, Deadline: 2, Novelty: tedious\n- Morning focus: 7, Afternoon: 5, Evening: 4, Hyperfocus: false, Task switch: 5, Emotional: 6, Words: 800, Crash: 4\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Vyvanse: 7:00, Protein: true, Coffee: 2, Vitamin C: none, Exercise: false\n- Sleep: 7, Deadline: 8, Novelty: tedious\n- Morning focus: 8, Afternoon: 7, Evening: 5, Hyperfocus: false, Task switch: 7, Emotional: 4, Words: 2400, Crash: 5\n\n**Friday:**\n- Vyvanse: 8:00, Protein: true, Coffee: 2, Vitamin C: evening, Exercise: true\n- Sleep: 7, Deadline: 9, Novelty: routine\n- Morning focus: 8, Afternoon: 8, Evening: 6, Hyperfocus: true, Task switch: 6, Emotional: 5, Words: 4100, Crash: 4\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Vyvanse: none (med break), Protein: n/a, Coffee: 1, Vitamin C: none, Exercise: false\n- Sleep: 10, Deadline: 0, Novelty: n/a\n- Morning focus: 2, Afternoon: 2, Evening: 3, Hyperfocus: false, Task switch: 2, Emotional: 4, Words: 0, Crash: 0\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Vyvanse: none (med break), Protein: n/a, Coffee: 0, Vitamin C: none, Exercise: true\n- Sleep: 9, Deadline: 0, Novelty: n/a\n- Morning focus: 3, Afternoon: 4, Evening: 4, Hyperfocus: false, Task switch: 3, Emotional: 6, Words: 200, Crash: 0\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Vyvanse before 7:30 + protein breakfast** → 35% higher morning focus scores\n2. **Vitamin C in morning** → 40% reduction in medication effectiveness (acidifies stomach, reduces absorption)\n3. **Exercise before work** → 25% improvement in task switching scores\n4. **Tedious tasks + low deadline pressure** → 60% reduction in output regardless of medication\n5. **Coffee >2 cups** → diminishing returns, evening crash increases by 20%\n6. **Sleep <6.5 hours** → 45% reduction in emotional regulation scores\n7. **Hyperfocus episodes** → 250% output but next-day crash severity +2 points\n8. **Med break days** → baseline focus 2-3, but emotional regulation often improves\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Task type confounding:** Marcus writes 4100 words on Friday not just because of medication, but because it's a new exciting project with high deadline. The model might over-attribute to medication.\n\n2. **Medication tolerance:** After 6 months, his baseline effectiveness drops. Need long-term trend analysis, not just daily correlations.\n\n3. **Hyperfocus is double-edged:** It produces great output but causes crashes and neglected responsibilities. The model should flag \"hyperfocus 3+ days in a row\" as a warning.\n\n4. **Weekend data gaps:** Med break days have fundamentally different patterns. Should they be excluded from correlations or analyzed separately?\n\n5. **Creative work variability:** Some days he can't write because he's doing research or editing, not because focus is poor. Need task-type context.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 7: Eczema (Atopic Dermatitis)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Priya, 29, Nurse  \n**Condition:** Moderate atopic dermatitis, on tacrolimus ointment and antihistamines PRN  \n**Goals:** Identify flare triggers, optimize skincare routine, reduce steroid dependence\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Shower duration | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Water temperature | input | select | - | cold, lukewarm, warm, hot |\n| Moisturizer applied | input | number | times | - |\n| Moisturizer within 3min of shower | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Tacrolimus used | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Antihistamine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Dairy consumed | input | select | - | none, trace, moderate, high |\n| Laundry detergent | input | select | - | fragrance-free, regular, new-brand |\n| Wool/synthetic worn | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Humidity | context | select | - | very-dry, dry, moderate, humid |\n| Work shift | context | select | - | day, night, off |\n| Itch intensity | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Redness | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Skin cracking | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Sleep disruption from itch | output | number | times-woken | - |\n| Affected areas | output | select | - | hands, elbows, neck, face, generalized |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Shower: 10, Temp: lukewarm, Moisturizer: 3, Within 3min: true, Tacrolimus: false, Antihistamine: false\n- Dairy: none, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 4, Humidity: moderate, Shift: day\n- Itch: 3, Redness: 3, Cracking: false, Sleep disruption: 0, Areas: hands\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Shower: 15, Temp: hot, Moisturizer: 2, Within 3min: false, Tacrolimus: false, Antihistamine: false\n- Dairy: moderate, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 6, Humidity: dry, Shift: night\n- Itch: 5, Redness: 5, Cracking: false, Sleep disruption: 1, Areas: hands, elbows\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Shower: 8, Temp: lukewarm, Moisturizer: 4, Within 3min: true, Tacrolimus: true, Antihistamine: true\n- Dairy: trace, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 7, Humidity: dry, Shift: night\n- Itch: 4, Redness: 4, Cracking: false, Sleep disruption: 1, Areas: hands, elbows\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Shower: 12, Temp: warm, Moisturizer: 2, Within 3min: false, Tacrolimus: true, Antihistamine: false\n- Dairy: high, Detergent: new-brand, Wool: true\n- Stress: 8, Humidity: very-dry, Shift: night\n- Itch: 8, Redness: 7, Cracking: true, Sleep disruption: 3, Areas: generalized\n\n**Friday:**\n- Shower: 6, Temp: lukewarm, Moisturizer: 5, Within 3min: true, Tacrolimus: true, Antihistamine: true\n- Dairy: none, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 5, Humidity: dry, Shift: off\n- Itch: 5, Redness: 5, Cracking: true, Sleep disruption: 1, Areas: hands, elbows, neck\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Shower: 8, Temp: lukewarm, Moisturizer: 4, Within 3min: true, Tacrolimus: true, Antihistamine: false\n- Dairy: none, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 3, Humidity: moderate, Shift: off\n- Itch: 3, Redness: 3, Cracking: false, Sleep disruption: 0, Areas: hands\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Shower: 10, Temp: lukewarm, Moisturizer: 4, Within 3min: true, Tacrolimus: false, Antihistamine: false\n- Dairy: none, Detergent: fragrance-free, Wool: false\n- Stress: 2, Humidity: moderate, Shift: off\n- Itch: 2, Redness: 2, Cracking: false, Sleep disruption: 0, Areas: hands\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Hot showers >10min** → 70% correlation with next-day itch increase of +2 points\n2. **Moisturizer within 3min of shower** → 40% reduction in overnight itch\n3. **Wool/synthetic clothing** → 65% correlation with generalized flare\n4. **Dairy high + stress >6** → synergistic effect, 80% chance of flare within 48 hours\n5. **Night shifts** → 55% correlation with worse symptoms (stress, disrupted routine, hospital irritants)\n6. **New detergent brand** → immediate flare trigger, 90% correlation\n7. **Humidity \"very-dry\" + low moisturizer (<3x)** → 75% correlation with skin cracking\n8. **3+ days off with routine skincare** → 85% correlation with symptom improvement\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Occupational exposures:** Priya washes hands 50+ times per shift at the hospital. This isn't captured and is a major driver of hand eczema.\n\n2. **Seasonal patterns:** Winter is always worse regardless of behavior. The model needs to account for baseline seasonal variation.\n\n3. **Delayed reactions:** A new detergent used on Monday might not cause a reaction until Wednesday when she wears those clothes. Need to track \"first time wearing newly washed clothes.\"\n\n4. **Topical steroid withdrawal:** If she uses steroids heavily then stops, there's a rebound flare. This pattern looks like \"stopping medication = worse\" when it's actually withdrawal.\n\n5. **Scratch-itch cycle:** Did she wake up itchy, or did she scratch in sleep which caused the itch? The causal direction is unclear.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 8: PCOS (Polycystic Ovary Syndrome)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Amanda, 31, Financial Analyst  \n**Condition:** PCOS with insulin resistance, irregular cycles, on metformin 500mg 2x daily  \n**Goals:** Regulate cycles, reduce acne, optimize fertility window identification\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Net carbs | input | number | grams | - |\n| Protein | input | number | grams | - |\n| Inositol supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Metformin taken | input | select | - | both-doses, one-dose, none |\n| Exercise type | input | select | - | hiit, strength, walking, yoga, none |\n| Exercise duration | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Dairy consumed | input | select | - | none, low-fat, full-fat |\n| Added sugar | input | number | grams | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Cycle day | context | number | day | - |\n| Bleeding | output | select | - | none, spotting, light, medium, heavy |\n| Acne severity | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Acne location | output | select | - | none, chin-jaw, forehead, cheeks, mixed |\n| Bloating | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Mood | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Cervical mucus | output | select | - | none, sticky, creamy, watery, egg-white |\n| Basal body temp | output | number | °F | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries (Mid-Cycle)\n\n**Monday (Cycle Day 14):**\n- Carbs: 45g, Protein: 100g, Inositol: true, Metformin: both, Exercise: strength 45min\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: 8g, Sleep: 7, Stress: 4\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 3, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 2, Mood: 7, Mucus: creamy, BBT: 97.4\n\n**Tuesday (Cycle Day 15):**\n- Carbs: 50g, Protein: 95g, Inositol: true, Metformin: both, Exercise: walking 30min\n- Dairy: full-fat, Sugar: 12g, Sleep: 7.5, Stress: 3\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 3, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 3, Mood: 7, Mucus: watery, BBT: 97.3\n\n**Wednesday (Cycle Day 16):**\n- Carbs: 40g, Protein: 110g, Inositol: true, Metformin: both, Exercise: yoga 40min\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: 5g, Sleep: 8, Stress: 2\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 2, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 2, Mood: 8, Mucus: egg-white, BBT: 97.2\n\n**Thursday (Cycle Day 17):**\n- Carbs: 55g, Protein: 90g, Inositol: true, Metformin: both, Exercise: none\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: 10g, Sleep: 6.5, Stress: 5\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 2, Location: none, Bloating: 2, Mood: 7, Mucus: egg-white, BBT: 97.5\n\n**Friday (Cycle Day 18):**\n- Carbs: 80g, Protein: 75g, Inositol: false, Metformin: one-dose, Exercise: hiit 25min\n- Dairy: full-fat, Sugar: 25g, Sleep: 6, Stress: 6\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 4, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 5, Mood: 5, Mucus: creamy, BBT: 97.8\n\n**Saturday (Cycle Day 19):**\n- Carbs: 100g, Protein: 60g, Inositol: false, Metformin: both, Exercise: none\n- Dairy: full-fat, Sugar: 40g, Sleep: 8, Stress: 4\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 5, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 6, Mood: 5, Mucus: sticky, BBT: 97.9\n\n**Sunday (Cycle Day 20):**\n- Carbs: 50g, Protein: 100g, Inositol: true, Metformin: both, Exercise: walking 45min\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: 8g, Sleep: 8, Stress: 3\n- Bleeding: none, Acne: 5, Location: chin-jaw, Bloating: 4, Mood: 6, Mucus: sticky, BBT: 98.0\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Net carbs >70g + sugar >20g** → 80% correlation with acne increase within 48-72 hours\n2. **Full-fat dairy** → 65% correlation with next-day bloating increase and chin acne\n3. **Egg-white mucus + BBT dip** → ovulation indicator (fertility window)\n4. **BBT rise >0.4°F sustained** → confirms ovulation occurred\n5. **HIIT exercise** → 40% correlation with delayed ovulation (stress response)\n6. **Consistent low-carb (<60g) for 7+ days** → 50% improvement in cycle regularity\n7. **Missed metformin + high carbs** → synergistic negative effect on bloating and mood\n8. **Inositol taken consistently** → 30% reduction in acne severity over 2-week rolling average\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Irregular cycles:** Amanda's cycles range from 28-60 days. \"Cycle day\" is less meaningful when ovulation might happen on day 14 or day 35. Need flexible phase tracking.\n\n2. **Anovulatory cycles:** Sometimes she doesn't ovulate at all. The BBT shift never comes. Model shouldn't force an ovulation prediction.\n\n3. **Supplement stacking:** She's also taking spearmint tea for androgens, vitamin D, and omega-3s. Tracking all of these is burdensome but might be necessary.\n\n4. **Weight fluctuation:** Her weight changes by 5 lbs through the cycle. Is that water retention or actual gain? Need to distinguish.\n\n5. **Fertility vs symptom goals:** The correlation engine should recognize she has dual goals (symptom management + cycle regularity) that might conflict (e.g., extreme low-carb helps acne but can suppress ovulation).\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 9: Fibromyalgia\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Robert, 47, Former Construction Supervisor (now on disability)  \n**Condition:** Fibromyalgia with chronic fatigue, on duloxetine 60mg and gabapentin 300mg 3x daily  \n**Goals:** Identify flare triggers, find sustainable activity level, reduce pain medication\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Duloxetine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Gabapentin doses | input | number | count | - |\n| Physical activity level | input | select | - | rest, light, moderate, heavy |\n| Activity duration | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Stretching done | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Epsom salt bath | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Caffeine | input | number | mg | - |\n| Alcohol units | input | number | units | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Sleep quality | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Weather change | context | select | - | stable, cold-front, warm-front, rain |\n| Barometric trend | context | select | - | stable, rising, falling |\n| Overall pain | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Tender points active | output | number | count (0-18) | - |\n| Fatigue | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Cognitive fog | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Morning stiffness duration | output | number | minutes | - |\n| Functional capacity | output | select | - | house-bound, limited-errands, normal-light, active |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 3, Activity: light 30min, Stretching: true, Bath: false\n- Caffeine: 100mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 6, Quality: 4, Weather: stable, Pressure: stable\n- Pain: 5, Tender points: 8, Fatigue: 6, Fog: 4, Stiffness: 45min, Function: limited-errands\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 3, Activity: moderate 60min, Stretching: true, Bath: true\n- Caffeine: 150mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 7, Quality: 5, Weather: stable, Pressure: stable\n- Pain: 4, Tender points: 6, Fatigue: 5, Fog: 3, Stiffness: 30min, Function: normal-light\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 3, Activity: heavy 90min, Stretching: false, Bath: false\n- Caffeine: 200mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 5, Quality: 3, Weather: cold-front, Pressure: falling\n- Pain: 7, Tender points: 12, Fatigue: 8, Fog: 6, Stiffness: 90min, Function: limited-errands\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 4 (extra for flare), Activity: rest, Stretching: false, Bath: true\n- Caffeine: 50mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 8, Quality: 4, Weather: rain, Pressure: falling\n- Pain: 8, Tender points: 14, Fatigue: 9, Fog: 7, Stiffness: 120min, Function: house-bound\n\n**Friday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 4, Activity: rest, Stretching: true (gentle), Bath: true\n- Caffeine: 75mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 9, Quality: 5, Weather: rain, Pressure: stable\n- Pain: 6, Tender points: 10, Fatigue: 7, Fog: 5, Stiffness: 75min, Function: limited-errands\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 3, Activity: light 20min, Stretching: true, Bath: false\n- Caffeine: 100mg, Alcohol: 2, Sleep: 7, Quality: 5, Weather: stable, Pressure: rising\n- Pain: 5, Tender points: 8, Fatigue: 6, Fog: 4, Stiffness: 45min, Function: limited-errands\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Duloxetine: true, Gabapentin: 3, Activity: light 25min, Stretching: true, Bath: true\n- Caffeine: 100mg, Alcohol: 0, Sleep: 7.5, Quality: 6, Weather: stable, Pressure: stable\n- Pain: 4, Tender points: 6, Fatigue: 5, Fog: 3, Stiffness: 30min, Function: normal-light\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Heavy activity** → 85% correlation with next-day flare (+3 pain, +50% stiffness duration) — \"boom and bust\" pattern\n2. **Barometric pressure falling + weather change** → 70% correlation with increased tender points\n3. **Epsom salt bath evening before** → 25% reduction in morning stiffness\n4. **Sleep quality <4** → 80% correlation with fog >5 and fatigue >6 regardless of hours slept\n5. **Stretching on consecutive days** → 20% reduction in baseline stiffness\n6. **Moderate activity (30-60min)** → optimal zone, no next-day flare in 70% of cases\n7. **Alcohol >1 unit** → 55% correlation with worse sleep quality and next-day pain increase\n8. **3+ rest days in a row** → paradoxically increases stiffness (deconditioning effect)\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Activity pacing dilemma:** The model should recognize that too much AND too little activity are both problematic. Need to find the \"Goldilocks zone.\"\n\n2. **Post-exertional malaise timing:** The crash from Wednesday's heavy activity didn't peak until Thursday. Need 24-72 hour lag analysis.\n\n3. **Medication side effects vs symptoms:** Gabapentin causes cognitive fog. Is today's fog from fibro or medication? Hard to disentangle.\n\n4. **Subjective scale reliability:** Robert's \"5\" pain on a good day might be another person's \"8.\" The model should focus on relative changes, not absolute numbers.\n\n5. **Unpredictable flares:** Some flares have no identifiable trigger. Robert needs validation that this is normal, not false patterns.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 10: Insomnia (Chronic Primary)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Christine, 52, Real Estate Agent  \n**Condition:** Chronic primary insomnia (10+ years), history of sleep medication dependence, currently medication-free  \n**Goals:** Fall asleep within 30 minutes, reduce middle-of-night waking, achieve 6+ hours consistently\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Caffeine cutoff time | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Last meal time | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Alcohol units | input | number | units | - |\n| Screen time before bed | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Blue light filter used | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Bedroom temperature | input | number | °F | - |\n| Magnesium taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Evening exercise | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Bedtime | context | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Work stress | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Naps | context | number | minutes | - |\n| Time to fall asleep | output | number | minutes | - |\n| Night wakings | output | number | count | - |\n| Time awake during night | output | number | minutes | - |\n| Final wake time | output | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Total sleep | output | number | hours | - |\n| Sleep quality subjective | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Next-day energy | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 14:00, Last meal: 19:00, Alcohol: 0, Screen: 60, Blue filter: false\n- Bedroom temp: 72, Magnesium: false, Evening exercise: false, Bedtime: 23:00, Stress: 6, Naps: 0\n- Fall asleep: 55, Wakings: 3, Awake time: 45, Final wake: 5:30, Total: 5.0, Quality: 3, Energy: 3\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 12:00, Last meal: 19:30, Alcohol: 2, Screen: 30, Blue filter: true\n- Bedroom temp: 70, Magnesium: true, Evening exercise: false, Bedtime: 22:30, Stress: 5, Naps: 0\n- Fall asleep: 35, Wakings: 4, Awake time: 60, Final wake: 5:00, Total: 4.5, Quality: 2, Energy: 2\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 13:00, Last meal: 18:30, Alcohol: 0, Screen: 45, Blue filter: true\n- Bedroom temp: 68, Magnesium: true, Evening exercise: true (6pm), Bedtime: 23:00, Stress: 4, Naps: 20\n- Fall asleep: 40, Wakings: 2, Awake time: 25, Final wake: 6:00, Total: 5.5, Quality: 5, Energy: 5\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 14:00, Last meal: 20:00, Alcohol: 0, Screen: 90, Blue filter: false\n- Bedroom temp: 73, Magnesium: false, Evening exercise: false, Bedtime: 23:30, Stress: 7, Naps: 0\n- Fall asleep: 70, Wakings: 4, Awake time: 55, Final wake: 5:15, Total: 4.0, Quality: 2, Energy: 2\n\n**Friday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 11:00, Last meal: 18:00, Alcohol: 0, Screen: 20, Blue filter: true\n- Bedroom temp: 67, Magnesium: true, Evening exercise: true (5pm), Bedtime: 22:00, Stress: 3, Naps: 0\n- Fall asleep: 25, Wakings: 1, Awake time: 15, Final wake: 6:00, Total: 7.0, Quality: 7, Energy: 7\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 12:00, Last meal: 21:00, Alcohol: 3, Screen: 60, Blue filter: false\n- Bedroom temp: 70, Magnesium: false, Evening exercise: false, Bedtime: 24:00, Stress: 2, Naps: 0\n- Fall asleep: 20, Wakings: 4, Awake time: 70, Final wake: 6:30, Total: 4.5, Quality: 3, Energy: 3\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Caffeine cutoff: 13:00, Last meal: 19:00, Alcohol: 0, Screen: 40, Blue filter: true\n- Bedroom temp: 68, Magnesium: true, Evening exercise: false, Bedtime: 22:30, Stress: 5 (anticipatory about Monday), Naps: 30\n- Fall asleep: 50, Wakings: 2, Awake time: 30, Final wake: 5:45, Total: 5.0, Quality: 4, Energy: 4\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Bedroom temp >70°F** → 45% increase in time to fall asleep\n2. **Alcohol** → falls asleep faster but 3x more night wakings and poor quality (fragmented sleep)\n3. **Screen time >60min without blue filter** → 50% increase in sleep onset latency\n4. **Caffeine cutoff before noon** → 35% improvement in sleep quality\n5. **Magnesium + temp <68°F** → 60% correlation with best sleep nights\n6. **Late meal (after 20:00)** → 40% more night wakings\n7. **Naps >20min** → 55% correlation with difficulty falling asleep that night\n8. **Consistent bedtime (within 30min window)** → 30% improvement in sleep quality over 2-week rolling average\n9. **Sunday night anticipatory stress** → 65% correlation with worst sleep of the week\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Sleep tracking anxiety:** Christine checks her tracker obsessively, which increases arousal and worsens sleep. The tracking itself becomes a trigger.\n\n2. **Paradoxical intention:** Some techniques (like \"trying to stay awake\") help insomnia. The model can't easily recommend counterintuitive interventions.\n\n3. **First-night effect:** When traveling (common for real estate), her first night is always terrible. Need a \"new sleep environment\" context flag.\n\n4. **Sleep state misperception:** She reports being awake for 2 hours but objective data might show 30 minutes. Subjective reporting is unreliable.\n\n5. **Weekend rhythm disruption:** Late nights Saturday shift her circadian rhythm, causing Sunday insomnia. This weekly pattern compounds.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 11: Rheumatoid Arthritis\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** David, 58, Retired Pharmacist  \n**Condition:** Rheumatoid arthritis (12 years), on methotrexate 15mg weekly + hydroxychloroquine daily  \n**Goals:** Reduce flare frequency, monitor medication side effects, maintain hand function for hobbies (woodworking)\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Methotrexate taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Hydroxychloroquine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Folic acid supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Fish oil dose | input | number | mg EPA+DHA | - |\n| Alcohol units | input | number | units | - |\n| Hand activity level | input | select | - | rest, light, moderate, heavy |\n| Anti-inflammatory foods | input | select | - | none, some, focused |\n| Omega-6 heavy foods | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Weather | context | select | - | clear, cloudy, rain, cold-snap |\n| Morning stiffness duration | output | number | minutes | - |\n| Hand pain L | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Hand pain R | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Grip strength subjective | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Swollen joint count | output | number | count | - |\n| Tender joint count | output | number | count | - |\n| Fatigue | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Nausea (MTX side effect) | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday (MTX day):**\n- MTX: true, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 2000, Alcohol: 0, Hand activity: light\n- Anti-inflam foods: focused, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 7, Stress: 3, Weather: clear\n- Stiffness: 30, Pain L: 3, Pain R: 4, Grip: 7, Swollen: 2, Tender: 4, Fatigue: 4, Nausea: 2\n\n**Tuesday (day after MTX):**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 2000, Alcohol: 0, Hand activity: rest\n- Anti-inflam foods: some, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 6, Stress: 3, Weather: clear\n- Stiffness: 45, Pain L: 4, Pain R: 5, Grip: 6, Swollen: 2, Tender: 5, Fatigue: 6, Nausea: 4\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 2000, Alcohol: 0, Hand activity: moderate (woodworking)\n- Anti-inflam foods: some, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 7, Stress: 2, Weather: cloudy\n- Stiffness: 25, Pain L: 3, Pain R: 4, Grip: 7, Swollen: 2, Tender: 3, Fatigue: 4, Nausea: 1\n\n**Thursday:**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 0 (ran out), Alcohol: 2, Hand activity: heavy (project deadline)\n- Anti-inflam foods: none, Omega-6: true, Sleep: 6, Stress: 5, Weather: rain\n- Stiffness: 50, Pain L: 5, Pain R: 6, Grip: 5, Swollen: 4, Tender: 6, Fatigue: 5, Nausea: 0\n\n**Friday:**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 0, Alcohol: 0, Hand activity: rest\n- Anti-inflam foods: focused, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 7.5, Stress: 4, Weather: rain\n- Stiffness: 60, Pain L: 6, Pain R: 7, Grip: 4, Swollen: 5, Tender: 7, Fatigue: 6, Nausea: 0\n\n**Saturday:**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 2000 (restocked), Alcohol: 1, Hand activity: light\n- Anti-inflam foods: some, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 8, Stress: 2, Weather: cold-snap\n- Stiffness: 55, Pain L: 5, Pain R: 6, Grip: 5, Swollen: 4, Tender: 6, Fatigue: 5, Nausea: 0\n\n**Sunday:**\n- MTX: false, HCQ: true, Folic: true, Fish oil: 2000, Alcohol: 0, Hand activity: light\n- Anti-inflam foods: focused, Omega-6: false, Sleep: 8, Stress: 2, Weather: clear\n- Stiffness: 35, Pain L: 4, Pain R: 4, Grip: 6, Swollen: 3, Tender: 4, Fatigue: 4, Nausea: 0\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Day after methotrexate** → 80% correlation with peak fatigue and nausea (expected, manageable)\n2. **Fish oil missed 2+ days** → 45% increase in inflammation markers (swelling, tenderness)\n3. **Heavy hand activity** → 70% correlation with next-day pain increase of +2 points\n4. **Weather \"rain\" or \"cold-snap\"** → 55% correlation with increased stiffness duration\n5. **Alcohol >1 unit on methotrexate day** → 60% increase in next-day nausea (liver stress)\n6. **Omega-6 heavy foods + stress >4** → synergistic inflammation trigger\n7. **Consistent fish oil + anti-inflammatory diet** → 25% reduction in baseline tender joint count over 4 weeks\n8. **Sleep <6.5 hours** → 40% correlation with next-day fatigue >5\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Medication cycling:** The MTX-day pattern creates predictable weekly variation. The model should recognize this as expected rather than problematic.\n\n2. **Lab result integration:** David gets CRP and ESR tested quarterly. These objective markers would help validate subjective tracking.\n\n3. **Disease progression vs triggers:** Over years, baseline worsening might be disease progression, not lifestyle. Need long-term trend separation.\n\n4. **Hobby-driven behavior:** David pushes through pain to finish woodworking projects, causing flares. The model should flag \"heavy activity despite high pain\" patterns.\n\n5. **Folic acid timing:** Taking folic acid same day as MTX reduces efficacy. Need day-of-week awareness.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 12: Treatment-Resistant Depression with Seasonal Pattern\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Katherine, 44, University Professor  \n**Condition:** Major depressive disorder (treatment-resistant, seasonal component), on bupropion 300mg + lamotrigine 200mg  \n**Goals:** Identify early warning signs, optimize light therapy, maintain functioning through winter\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Bupropion taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Lamotrigine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Light therapy minutes | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Light therapy time | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Outdoor time | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Exercise | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Social interactions | input | number | count | - |\n| Alcohol units | input | number | units | - |\n| Omega-3 supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Wake time consistency | context | select | - | consistent, +/-1hr, +/-2hr, erratic |\n| Month | context | number | month | - |\n| Work deadlines | context | boolean | - | - |\n| Mood (morning) | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Mood (evening) | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Anhedonia | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Energy | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Concentration | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Suicidal ideation | output | select | - | none, passive, active-no-plan, active-with-plan |\n| Able to work | output | boolean | - | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries (January)\n\n**Monday:**\n- Bupropion: true, Lamotrigine: true, Light therapy: 30 at 7:00, Outdoor: 15\n- Exercise: 0, Social: 2, Alcohol: 0, Omega-3: true, Sleep: 9, Wake: consistent, Deadlines: true\n- Mood AM: 4, PM: 5, Anhedonia: 6, Energy: 3, Concentration: 4, SI: passive, Work: true\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Bupropion: true, Lamotrigine: true, Light therapy: 30 at 7:00, Outdoor: 10\n- Exercise: 20, Social: 3, Alcohol: 0, Omega-3: true, Sleep: 8, Wake: consistent, Deadlines: true\n- Mood AM: 5, P