### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Alcohol + spicy food same day** → 80% correlation with night sweats >2\n2. **Bedroom temp >68°F + no wicking sleepwear** → 65% more night sweats\n3. **Exercise >30min** → 40% reduction in next-day hot flash frequency\n4. **Caffeine >100mg** → 35% increase in hot flash severity\n5. **Phytoestrogen \"focused\" consistently** → 25% reduction in hot flash count over 2-week rolling average\n6. **Work stress >6** → 50% correlation with anxiety >5 and mood <5\n7. **Black cohosh skipped 3+ days** → gradual return of symptoms over 5-7 days\n8. **Sleep <6 hours** → 45% correlation with worse mood and anxiety regardless of other factors\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Irregular periods:** \"Days since last period\" is unreliable when periods are 28-90 days apart. Need phase detection beyond simple counting.\n\n2. **Supplement effectiveness lag:** Black cohosh takes 4-8 weeks to show effects. Short-term correlations are misleading.\n\n3. **Hot flash vs panic attack:** Some \"hot flashes\" might actually be anxiety responses. Symptoms overlap significantly.\n\n4. **Temperature subjectivity:** Her partner sets thermostat to 72°F. She changes it to 65°F. Recording \"bedroom temperature\" depends on who checked last.\n\n5. **Symptom cluster interdependence:** Poor sleep → worse mood → more anxiety → more hot flashes → worse sleep. Breaking the cycle requires multi-factor intervention.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 15: Chronic Low Back Pain\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Anthony, 45, Long-Haul Truck Driver  \n**Condition:** Chronic mechanical low back pain (degenerative disc L4-L5, L5-S1), no surgery  \n**Goals:** Reduce pain medication, maintain ability to work, prevent acute flares\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Driving hours | input | number | hours | - |\n| Breaks taken | input | number | count | - |\n| Stretching done | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Core exercises | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Walking | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Ibuprofen doses | input | number | count | - |\n| Muscle relaxant taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Ice applied | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Heat applied | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Ergonomic cushion used | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Sleep position | context | select | - | back, side-pillow, side-no-pillow, stomach |\n| Mattress firmness | context | select | - | hotel-soft, hotel-firm, home |\n| Lifting done | context | select | - | none, light, heavy |\n| Morning pain | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Evening pain | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Pain radiation | output | select | - | none, buttock, thigh, below-knee |\n| Stiffness | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Numbness/tingling | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Functional limitation | output | select | - | none, mild, moderate, severe |\n| Missed work | output | boolean | - | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries (On the Road)\n\n**Monday:**\n- Driving: 10, Breaks: 3, Stretching: true, Core: 0, Walking: 15\n- Ibuprofen: 2, Relaxant: false, Ice: false, Heat: true, Cushion: true\n- Sleep: side-pillow, Mattress: home, Lifting: light\n- AM pain: 4, PM pain: 6, Radiation: buttock, Stiffness: 5, Numbness: false, Function: mild, Missed: false\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Driving: 11, Breaks: 2, Stretching: false, Core: 0, Walking: 10\n- Ibuprofen: 3, Relaxant: false, Ice: false, Heat: true, Cushion: true\n- Sleep: side-pillow, Mattress: hotel-soft, Lifting: none\n- AM pain: 5, PM pain: 7, Radiation: thigh, Stiffness: 6, Numbness: false, Function: moderate, Missed: false\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Driving: 11, Breaks: 2, Stretching: false, Core: 0, Walking: 5\n- Ibuprofen: 4, Relaxant: true, Ice: true, Heat: false, Cushion: true\n- Sleep: side-no-pillow, Mattress: hotel-firm, Lifting: heavy (unexpected)\n- AM pain: 6, PM pain: 8, Radiation: below-knee, Stiffness: 7, Numbness: true, Function: moderate, Missed: false\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Driving: 8, Breaks: 4, Stretching: true, Core: 10, Walking: 20\n- Ibuprofen: 3, Relaxant: true, Ice: true, Heat: true, Cushion: true\n- Sleep: side-pillow, Mattress: hotel-firm, Lifting: none\n- AM pain: 6, PM pain: 6, Radiation: thigh, Stiffness: 5, Numbness: true, Function: moderate, Missed: false\n\n**Friday:**\n- Driving: 6, Breaks: 3, Stretching: true, Core: 15, Walking: 30\n- Ibuprofen: 2, Relaxant: false, Ice: false, Heat: true, Cushion: true\n- Sleep: side-pillow, Mattress: hotel-firm, Lifting: none\n- AM pain: 5, PM pain: 5, Radiation: buttock, Stiffness: 4, Numbness: false, Function: mild, Missed: false\n\n**Saturday (home):**\n- Driving: 0, Breaks: n/a, Stretching: true, Core: 20, Walking: 45\n- Ibuprofen: 1, Relaxant: false, Ice: false, Heat: false, Cushion: n/a\n- Sleep: side-pillow, Mattress: home, Lifting: light\n- AM pain: 4, PM pain: 3, Radiation: none, Stiffness: 3, Numbness: false, Function: none, Missed: false\n\n**Sunday (home):**\n- Driving: 0, Breaks: n/a, Stretching: true, Core: 20, Walking: 60\n- Ibuprofen: 0, Relaxant: false, Ice: false, Heat: false, Cushion: n/a\n- Sleep: back, Mattress: home, Lifting: none\n- AM pain: 3, PM pain: 2, Radiation: none, Stiffness: 2, Numbness: false, Function: none, Missed: false\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Driving >10 hours** → 75% correlation with evening pain >6\n2. **Breaks <3 per shift** → 55% correlation with radiation symptoms\n3. **Hotel soft mattress** → 65% correlation with next-morning pain increase +1-2 points\n4. **Core exercises + stretching consistently** → 30% reduction in weekly pain average\n5. **Heavy lifting** → 85% correlation with below-knee radiation within 24 hours (nerve irritation)\n6. **Side sleeping without pillow** → 40% more morning stiffness\n7. **Walking >30min on non-driving days** → 35% improvement in baseline\n8. **Ibuprofen >3 doses/day for 3+ days** → 20% less effective (tolerance) + GI symptoms\n9. **Numbness/tingling appearing** → 70% correlation with prior heavy lifting or driving >10hr\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Occupational constraints:** Anthony can't just \"take more breaks\" — he has delivery schedules. Recommendations need to be practical within his job requirements.\n\n2. **Red flag symptoms:** Numbness below the knee could indicate nerve compression requiring medical attention. The model should flag these for escalation.\n\n3. **Hotel mattress lottery:** He can't control mattress firmness on the road. Maybe track hotel chain preferences that tend to have firmer mattresses.\n\n4. **Medication masking:** Higher ibuprofen doses might mask worsening symptoms. Need to track \"pain at same medication level\" for true trend.\n\n5. **Cumulative driving time:** It's not just daily hours — 5 consecutive 10+ hour days is worse than the same total spread out.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 16: Adult ADHD with Comorbid Anxiety\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Samantha, 26, Graduate Student  \n**Condition:** ADHD-Inattentive with comorbid generalized anxiety, on Strattera 60mg  \n**Goals:** Balance focus improvement without worsening anxiety, manage academic demands, improve time blindness\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Strattera taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Strattera timing | input | number | hour (24h) | - |\n| Caffeine mg | input | number | mg | - |\n| L-theanine supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Study technique | input | select | - | pomodoro, free-flow, body-doubling, none |\n| Exercise | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Nature exposure | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Phone pickup count | input | number | count | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Deadlines today | context | number | count | - |\n| Social obligations | context | number | count | - |\n| Focus quality | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Task initiation | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Anxiety level | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Time estimation accuracy | output | select | - | way-off, somewhat-off, close, accurate |\n| Emotional dysregulation episodes | output | number | count | - |\n| Procrastination hours | output | number | hours | - |\n| Productive output | output | select | - | nothing, minimal, moderate, good, excellent |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries (Finals Season)\n\n**Monday:**\n- Strattera: true at 8:00, Caffeine: 200, L-theanine: true, Technique: pomodoro, Exercise: 20\n- Nature: 10, Phone pickups: 45, Sleep: 6, Deadlines: 2, Social: 1\n- Focus: 5, Initiation: 4, Anxiety: 7, Time estimation: way-off, Dysregulation: 2, Procrastination: 3, Output: minimal\n\n**Tuesday:**\n- Strattera: true at 7:30, Caffeine: 100, L-theanine: true, Technique: body-doubling, Exercise: 0\n- Nature: 5, Phone pickups: 30, Sleep: 5.5, Deadlines: 2, Social: 0\n- Focus: 6, Initiation: 5, Anxiety: 6, Time estimation: somewhat-off, Dysregulation: 1, Procrastination: 2, Output: moderate\n\n**Wednesday:**\n- Strattera: true at 8:00, Caffeine: 150, L-theanine: false, Technique: free-flow, Exercise: 30\n- Nature: 30, Phone pickups: 25, Sleep: 7, Deadlines: 1, Social: 0\n- Focus: 7, Initiation: 6, Anxiety: 4, Time estimation: close, Dysregulation: 0, Procrastination: 1, Output: good\n\n**Thursday:**\n- Strattera: false (forgot), Caffeine: 250, L-theanine: false, Technique: none, Exercise: 0\n- Nature: 0, Phone pickups: 80, Sleep: 5, Deadlines: 1 (imminent), Social: 0\n- Focus: 3, Initiation: 2, Anxiety: 8, Time estimation: way-off, Dysregulation: 3, Procrastination: 5, Output: minimal\n\n**Friday:**\n- Strattera: true at 9:00 (late), Caffeine: 300, L-theanine: true, Technique: pomodoro, Exercise: 0\n- Nature: 0, Phone pickups: 55, Sleep: 4, Deadlines: 1 (submitted late), Social: 1\n- Focus: 4, Initiation: 5 (crisis mode), Anxiety: 9, Time estimation: somewhat-off, Dysregulation: 2, Procrastination: 2, Output: moderate (hyperfocus)\n\n**Saturday:**\n- Strattera: true at 10:00, Caffeine: 100, L-theanine: true, Technique: none, Exercise: 45\n- Nature: 60, Phone pickups: 20, Sleep: 9, Deadlines: 0, Social: 2\n- Focus: 6, Initiation: 5, Anxiety: 3, Time estimation: close, Dysregulation: 0, Procrastination: 2, Output: moderate\n\n**Sunday:**\n- Strattera: true at 8:30, Caffeine: 75, L-theanine: true, Technique: body-doubling, Exercise: 30\n- Nature: 45, Phone pickups: 15, Sleep: 8, Deadlines: 0, Social: 1\n- Focus: 7, Initiation: 6, Anxiety: 4, Time estimation: accurate, Dysregulation: 0, Procrastination: 1, Output: good\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Missed Strattera** → 60% correlation with focus <4, phone pickups >60, emotional dysregulation episodes >2\n2. **Caffeine >200mg without L-theanine** → 70% correlation with anxiety >6\n3. **Nature exposure >30min** → 40% improvement in time estimation accuracy\n4. **Body-doubling technique** → 50% better task initiation than solo techniques\n5. **Phone pickups >50** → 80% correlation with procrastination >3 hours and output <moderate\n6. **Sleep <6 hours + deadlines >1** → 85% correlation with emotional dysregulation\n7. **Exercise + nature same day** → lowest anxiety scores of the week\n8. **Strattera taken after 9am** → 35% reduction in afternoon effectiveness\n9. **Crisis-mode hyperfocus** → produces output but correlates with next-day burnout\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Medication and caffeine interaction:** The model should recognize that high caffeine is often self-medication for ADHD symptoms, especially when Strattera is missed.\n\n2. **Productivity vs wellbeing tradeoff:** Friday's \"moderate\" output came at the cost of anxiety 9 and 4 hours of sleep. The model should flag unsustainable patterns.\n\n3. **Time blindness tracking:** Asking someone with time blindness to accurately report hours is inherently unreliable. Consider external validation (screen time data).\n\n4. **Emotional dysregulation defining:** Is crying from frustration one episode or multiple? Need clear definitions.\n\n5. **Procrastination as symptom:** Procrastination isn't a choice for ADHD — it's task avoidance driven by executive dysfunction. The model shouldn't frame recommendations as \"just try harder.\"\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 17: Lupus (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Michelle, 39, Lawyer (part-time due to illness)  \n**Condition:** SLE with joint involvement, photosensitivity, fatigue; on hydroxychloroquine + low-dose prednisone  \n**Goals:** Minimize flares, optimize energy for work days, reduce steroid dependence\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Hydroxychloroquine taken | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Prednisone dose | input | number | mg | - |\n| Sun exposure | input | number | minutes | - |\n| Sunscreen applied | input | boolean | - | - |\n| SPF clothing worn | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Anti-inflammatory diet adherence | input | select | - | poor, moderate, good, excellent |\n| Vitamin D supplement | input | number | IU | - |\n| Rest hours (non-sleep) | input | number | hours | - |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| UV index | context | number | index | - |\n| Work day | context | boolean | - | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Fatigue | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Joint pain | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Malar rash present | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Photosensitivity reaction | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Brain fog | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n| Fever | output | boolean | - | - |\n| Hair shedding | output | select | - | normal, mild, moderate, severe |\n| Mouth ulcers | output | boolean | - | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday (work day):**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 5mg, Sun: 20, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: true\n- Diet: good, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 1, Sleep: 7, UV: 6, Work: true, Stress: 6\n- Fatigue: 5, Joint: 4, Rash: false, Photosensitivity: false, Fog: 3, Fever: false, Hair: normal, Ulcers: false\n\n**Tuesday (work day):**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 5mg, Sun: 45, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: false\n- Diet: moderate, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 0.5, Sleep: 6, UV: 8, Work: true, Stress: 7\n- Fatigue: 6, Joint: 5, Rash: true, Photosensitivity: true, Fog: 4, Fever: false, Hair: normal, Ulcers: false\n\n**Wednesday (rest day):**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 7.5mg (increased for flare), Sun: 5, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: true\n- Diet: excellent, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 3, Sleep: 9, UV: 5, Work: false, Stress: 4\n- Fatigue: 5, Joint: 5, Rash: true, Photosensitivity: false, Fog: 4, Fever: false, Hair: mild, Ulcers: false\n\n**Thursday (rest day):**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 7.5mg, Sun: 0, Sunscreen: n/a, SPF clothing: n/a\n- Diet: good, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 4, Sleep: 8, UV: 7, Work: false, Stress: 3\n- Fatigue: 4, Joint: 4, Rash: false, Photosensitivity: false, Fog: 3, Fever: false, Hair: mild, Ulcers: false\n\n**Friday (work day):**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 5mg, Sun: 30, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: true\n- Diet: good, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 0.5, Sleep: 7, UV: 6, Work: true, Stress: 5\n- Fatigue: 5, Joint: 4, Rash: false, Photosensitivity: false, Fog: 3, Fever: false, Hair: normal, Ulcers: false\n\n**Saturday:**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 5mg, Sun: 60, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: false\n- Diet: moderate, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 2, Sleep: 8, UV: 9, Work: false, Stress: 4\n- Fatigue: 6, Joint: 5, Rash: true, Photosensitivity: true, Fog: 4, Fever: false, Hair: mild, Ulcers: true\n\n**Sunday:**\n- HCQ: true, Prednisone: 7.5mg (flare), Sun: 10, Sunscreen: true, SPF clothing: true\n- Diet: excellent, Vitamin D: 2000IU, Rest: 4, Sleep: 9, UV: 6, Work: false, Stress: 3\n- Fatigue: 5, Joint: 4, Rash: true, Photosensitivity: false, Fog: 3, Fever: false, Hair: mild, Ulcers: true\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Sun >30min + UV >7 + no SPF clothing** → 90% correlation with photosensitivity reaction within 24 hours\n2. **Photosensitivity reaction** → 80% correlation with malar rash appearing same or next day\n3. **Work days with stress >6** → 55% correlation with next-day fatigue >5\n4. **Rest hours <1 on work days** → 60% correlation with fog >4\n5. **Prednisone increase** → symptoms improve within 24-48hr but predict hair shedding 2-3 weeks later\n6. **Excellent diet adherence for 3+ days** → 25% reduction in joint pain scores\n7. **Mouth ulcers often precede flares** → 70% correlation with symptom worsening in next 48 hours (prodrome)\n8. **Sleep <7 hours + work day** → 75% correlation with needing extra rest day within 3 days\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **UV index tracking:** Michelle can't easily track UV index manually. Needs weather API integration or location-based auto-fill.\n\n2. **Steroid dependence spiral:** Higher prednisone relieves symptoms short-term but causes long-term problems. The model should track cumulative steroid exposure and flag trends.\n\n3. **Flare definition:** Is a malar rash appearing a \"flare\" or just a symptom? Need clear criteria for distinguishing daily variation from disease flare.\n\n4. **Prodrome recognition:** Mouth ulcers appearing Saturday might have predicted Sunday's worsening. The model should learn to recognize early warning patterns specific to her.\n\n5. **Work capacity planning:** She needs to predict which days she'll be functional for work. The model should help identify \"safe to schedule work\" conditions.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 18: Hormonal Acne (Adult Female)\n\n### User Profile\n**Name:** Jessica, 27, Marketing Coordinator  \n**Condition:** Hormonal acne (jawline/chin), history of PCOS, not on hormonal birth control  \n**Goals:** Clear skin without Accutane, identify triggers, manage cyclical breakouts\n\n### Items Tracked\n\n| Name | Type | Value Type | Unit | Options |\n|------|------|------------|------|---------|\n| Dairy consumed | input | select | - | none, low-fat, full-fat, whey-protein |\n| Sugar intake | input | select | - | low, moderate, high |\n| High-glycemic foods | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Zinc supplement | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Spearmint tea | input | number | cups | - |\n| New skincare product | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Pillowcase changed | input | boolean | - | - |\n| Face touching estimate | input | select | - | minimal, moderate, frequent |\n| Sleep hours | context | number | hours | - |\n| Cycle day | context | number | day | - |\n| Stress level | context | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Humidity | context | select | - | dry, moderate, humid |\n| New pimples | output | number | count | - |\n| Cystic acne | output | number | count | - |\n| Active breakout areas | output | select | - | none, chin, jawline, cheeks, forehead, mixed |\n| Healing pimples | output | number | count | - |\n| Skin oiliness | output | scale | 1-10 | - |\n| Inflammation level | output | scale | 0-10 | - |\n\n### Sample Week of Entries\n\n**Monday (Cycle Day 21 - Luteal Phase):**\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: low, High-glycemic: false, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 2\n- New product: false, Pillowcase: true, Face touching: minimal\n- Sleep: 7, Cycle: 21, Stress: 4, Humidity: moderate\n- New pimples: 1, Cystic: 0, Areas: chin, Healing: 2, Oiliness: 5, Inflammation: 3\n\n**Tuesday (Cycle Day 22):**\n- Dairy: full-fat, Sugar: moderate, High-glycemic: true, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 1\n- New product: false, Pillowcase: false, Face touching: moderate\n- Sleep: 6, Cycle: 22, Stress: 5, Humidity: moderate\n- New pimples: 2, Cystic: 0, Areas: chin-jawline, Healing: 2, Oiliness: 6, Inflammation: 4\n\n**Wednesday (Cycle Day 23):**\n- Dairy: whey-protein, Sugar: moderate, High-glycemic: true, Zinc: false, Spearmint: 0\n- New product: false, Pillowcase: false, Face touching: frequent\n- Sleep: 6, Cycle: 23, Stress: 6, Humidity: moderate\n- New pimples: 3, Cystic: 1, Areas: jawline, Healing: 3, Oiliness: 7, Inflammation: 5\n\n**Thursday (Cycle Day 24):**\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: low, High-glycemic: false, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 2\n- New product: false, Pillowcase: true, Face touching: minimal\n- Sleep: 7.5, Cycle: 24, Stress: 4, Humidity: moderate\n- New pimples: 1, Cystic: 1, Areas: jawline, Healing: 4, Oiliness: 6, Inflammation: 5\n\n**Friday (Cycle Day 25):**\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: low, High-glycemic: false, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 2\n- New product: true (new moisturizer), Pillowcase: false, Face touching: moderate\n- Sleep: 7, Cycle: 25, Stress: 5, Humidity: humid\n- New pimples: 2, Cystic: 0, Areas: chin-cheeks, Healing: 4, Oiliness: 7, Inflammation: 4\n\n**Saturday (Cycle Day 26):**\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: moderate, High-glycemic: false, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 1\n- New product: true (same), Pillowcase: false, Face touching: minimal\n- Sleep: 8, Cycle: 26, Stress: 3, Humidity: humid\n- New pimples: 3, Cystic: 0, Areas: cheeks, Healing: 5, Oiliness: 6, Inflammation: 4\n\n**Sunday (Cycle Day 27):**\n- Dairy: none, Sugar: low, High-glycemic: false, Zinc: true, Spearmint: 2\n- New product: false (discontinued), Pillowcase: true, Face touching: minimal\n- Sleep: 8, Cycle: 27, Stress: 2, Humidity: moderate\n- New pimples: 1, Cystic: 0, Areas: chin, Healing: 6, Oiliness: 5, Inflammation: 3\n\n### Correlations Engine Might Discover\n\n1. **Cycle days 21-28** → baseline 50% increase in new pimples compared to days 1-14\n2. **Whey protein** → 80% correlation with cystic acne within 48-72 hours\n3. **Full-fat dairy + high-glycemic same day** → 70% correlation with inflammation increase\n4. **New skincare product** → cheek breakouts (different location = product reaction vs hormonal)\n5. **Spearmint tea 2+ cups daily** → 25% reduction in oiliness over 2-week average\n6. **Pillowcase unchanged >3 days** → 40% more new pimples\n7. **Zinc supplement consistently** → 20% reduction in cystic acne frequency\n8. **Stress >6 + luteal phase** → synergistic effect, 85% correlation with worst breakouts\n\n### Edge Cases and Challenges\n\n1. **Breakout location matters:** Chin/jawline = hormonal, cheeks = product or mechanical irritation, forehead = different triggers. The model should correlate triggers with specific locations.\n\n2. **Delayed appearance:** Food consumed Tuesday may cause a pimple that appears Thursday. Need 48-72 hour lag analysis for dietary triggers.\n\n3. **Healing vs new confusion:** A cystic pimple from last week is still visible. Is she counting it as \"active\" or \"healing\"? Need clear definitions.\n\n4. **Product purging:** A new active ingredient might cause initial breakouts before improvement. The model might incorrectly flag beneficial products as triggers.\n\n5. **Hormonal variation:** Some cycles are worse than others based on ovulation strength, stress, etc. Need cycle-over-cycle comparison, not just day-to-day.\n\n---\n\n## Summary: Cross-Cutting Patterns and Design Recommendations\n\n### Common Challenges Across Conditions\n\n1. **Time Lag Variability:** Different conditions have different lag times (IBS: 24-48hr, thyroid: 4-6 weeks, acne: 48-72hr). The correlation engine needs condition-specific lag windows.\n\n2. **Non-Linear Relationships:** Thresholds, U-curves (too little and too much exercise both harmful), and synergistic effects require more than linear correlation models.\n\n3. **Baseline Drift:** Users recalibrate their subjective scales over time. Relative change is more reliable than absolute values.\n\n4. **Missing Data Patterns:** Sickest days often have least tracking. Missing data is informative.\n\n5. **Context Dependency:** The same input has different effects depending on other factors (alcohol + medication, food + stress).\n\n### Recommended Model Enhancements\n\n| Feature | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| Configurable lag windows | Allow 0-168 hour (1 week) lag analysis per condition |\n| Combination trigger detection | Identify 2-3 factor combinations that create synergistic effects |\n| Rolling baseline | Compare to 14-day rolling average, not static \"normal\" |\n| Trend alerts | Detect gradual worsening over 2-4 weeks |\n| Phase-aware analysis | Support menstrual cycle, medication cycles, weekly patterns |\n| Crisis detection | Flag specific combinations that warrant medical attention |\n| Uncertainty modeling | Track confidence in correlations based on data quality and volume |\n\n### Items Model Extension Suggestions\n\n```typescript\ninterface ExtendedItem {\n  name: string;\n  type: 'input' | 'output' | 'context';\n  value_type: 'number' | 'boolean' | 'scale' | 'text' | 'select' | 'time';\n  unit?: string;\n  options?: string[];\n  \n  // Extensions\n  lag_window_hours?: number;      // How far back to look for correlations\n  threshold_alert?: number;       // Value that triggers a warning\n  combination_with?: string[];    // Other items this commonly interacts with\n  prodrome_for?: string[];        // Outputs this might predict\n  data_source?: 'manual' | 'integration' | 'derived';\n  reliability?: 'objective' | 'subjective' | 'estimated';\n}\n```\n\nThis comprehensive scenario set provides a foundation for building a correlation engine that handles the real complexity of chronic health conditions."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11653,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11653},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CXnMS66PsBdULHLbwFNE3","type":"assistant","uuid":"bac77fd5-8fef-479f-9f84-840d91dc4fca","timestamp":"2026-02-04T04:01:33.072Z"}
